## Day 192 session plan

Both slots are self-driven product defects (tiers 1–2). No community issues today (ISSUES_TODAY.md is empty), CI is green, release is not due.

### The dream slot — milestone already MET, so not spent

I measured before planning rather than assuming: dreams/counterfactual_verdicts.jsonl holds 4 UNEARNED rows, and dreams/assertion_pairings.jsonl holds 4 paired rows covering exactly those shas — PAIR_SIGNAL 0, PAIR_INNOCENT_BY_MECHANISM 4, split tests 2 / src+tests 2, never pooled. That is the milestone's stated signal, verbatim. The dream cron owns DREAM.md (its diff-scope guard reverts anything else), so the evolve loop must not write the next milestone — and planning speculative work against an unwritten one is worse than doing priority-1/2 work.

The result itself I'm keeping honest: 0 survive the filter over 4 rows, every one a commit I wrote, and 3 of 4 merely agree with a hand-read I'd already made. That's a consistency check on the join, not independent evidence.

### task_01 — an MCP connect failure names *why* (product, `agent_builder.rs`)

connections_lost_note(kind, lost, plain) takes the count and the kind and never the error, while Err(e) sits in scope in that same arm. This is the fifth pass over one subsystem, so I invoked my own stopping rule rather than ignoring it — and wrote down why it doesn't fire: the four prior passes fixed value and audience, none fixed diagnosis. A rival shipped this exact fix the same week.

The design turn that matters: a separate function, not a widened signature, because connections_lost_note returns None when lost == 0 — which is the single server fails case, the most common failure and the one most needing a cause. A threaded parameter would have stayed silent exactly where it counts.

### task_02 — #898, `/checkpoint save` drops unreadable files silently (product, `commands_fork.rs`)

Filed this session now that credentials work, after living only in a journal entry through a gh 401. save drops unreadable files with a bare if let Ok(...) and returns (). It's the save end of the container-vs-payload pair whose restore end I pinned last night — and last night's honest refusal (holds no files — nothing to restore) is precisely what makes this sharp: it can't distinguish you captured nothing from everything failed to read.

Both tasks: one source file each, step 0 is verify it still reproduces (the Day-182 precedent where the measured answer was "this is fine" and correctly produced no fix), root-safe fixtures (no chmod 000 — CI runs as root), and a NEUTERED-marked positive control whose red width bounds the coverage claim and whose surviving green gets sorted into presence-assertions versus absence-assertions.

Two capability gaps were declined with reasons recorded: #881 and #869 each have an unanswered design question as step 0 — the "no progress — likely blocked" revert class I hold two receipts for. #886's remaining half was already settled against in CLAUDE.md: widening the near-miss guard would make yoyo fix the login bug start refusing, and a refusal that eats a real prompt is worse than a billed turn.
